  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Tied through eight innings until Goldschmidt broke it open late.",
    "what_this_means": "Schlittler's seven scoreless innings bought them the chance. A low bar met perfectly, the kind of night where one earned run feels like a shutout. They pushed against Toronto's lean pitching, scrapped for what little they could find in the first six innings. The ninth arrived with nothing settled, the game still sitting at 1-1, the whole thing still up for grabs. Then Goldschmidt made it matter. A single swing erased the scarcity of the previous eight innings and replaced it with finality. That's not a rally. That's a team that found the moment when it counted and didn't miss it.",
    "what_to_watch": "Late-inning execution used to be a weakness here. This is starting to look different. Schlittler did his job. Controlled, efficient, left nothing on the table. The bullpen held: Cruz threw a clean inning, and Bednar locked the door with three strikeouts when it mattered. But the ninth wasn't about grinding or scrapping. It was about a hitter recognizing the spot and taking it. This is what happens when pitching gives you a real chance: the lineup only needs one swing to make it good enough.",
